[FINAL-TEXT] The root cause is now confirmed. Look at r12's `ip addr show`:

- **`lo` (UP):** `102.1.1.1/24` → EIGRP topology shows it as Connected ✅
- **`lo1` (DOWN):** `102.1.2.1/24` → absent from r12's EIGRP topology ❌
- **`lo2` (DOWN):** `102.1.3.1/24` → absent from r12's EIGRP topology ❌
- **`lo3` (DOWN):** `102.1.4.1/24` → absent from r12's EIGRP topology ❌

r12's `frr.conf` comment even spells it out: *"Each loopback /24 sits on its own dummy interface (see r12.startup) so FRR eigrpd picks them all up."* The dummy interfaces lo1–lo3 are administratively down, so FRR EIGRP never matches those `network` statements and never originates those three prefixes into the EIGRP topology. r11's EIGRP topology entries for them are transient/stale holdover state. Once EIGRP fully converges, r11 will not have those routes to redistribute into OSPF, and r1's RIB will be missing them — exactly the reported symptom.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r12
diagnosis_text:
The three missing prefixes (102.1.2.0/24, 102.1.3.0/24, 102.1.4.0/24) are hosted on r12's dummy loopback interfaces lo1, lo2, and lo3 respectively. All three interfaces are administratively DOWN (confirmed via `ip addr show` on r12: state DOWN for lo1/lo2/lo3, while lo carrying 102.1.1.1/24 is UP). FRR's EIGRP daemon activates a `network` statement only when a matching interface is operationally up; because lo1–lo3 are down, the three corresponding /24 connected routes are never inserted into r12's EIGRP topology table. This is directly confirmed: r12's EIGRP topology shows only `102.1.1.0/24 via Connected, lo`, with no entries for 102.1.2–4.0/24. Since r12 does not originate those prefixes, r11 never receives them via EIGRP AS 100 (the EE/112.x segment), never installs them in its routing table, and never redistributes them into OSPF area 3 toward r1. The EIGRP adjacency between r12 and r11 remains fully up because the fault is on r12's loopback dummy interfaces, not the physical eth0 link — explaining why the adjacency is healthy yet the routes are absent. The root cause is that r12's startup configuration fails to bring lo1, lo2, and lo3 to an UP state before (or at the time) FRR starts. The fix is to execute `ip link set lo1 up; ip link set lo2 up; ip link set lo3 up` on r12 and add those commands permanently to r12's startup script so they are applied before FRR is launched.
=== END ===